{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":253528,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"7f271338-4201-4a83-a57f-66689d7be786","total_cost_usd":1.284716,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":69250,"cache_read_input_tokens":20672,"output_tokens":21214,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":69250,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":21214,"cache_read_input_tokens":20672,"cache_creation_input_tokens":69250,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":69250},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":51420,"outputTokens":20,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.05152,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":21214,"cacheReadInputTokens":20672,"cacheCreationInputTokens":69250,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.233196,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"spec-auditor\",\n  \"verdict\": \"fail\",\n  \"summary\": \"The catalog is clean and internally consistent, but the parameter set fails six checks rooted in one decision: a 105 kg dry mass chosen to satisfy the full-hold-doubling rule puts the staged ballistic coefficient at 21.4, crashes every ascent at ~5.1 km, and leaves Armstrong's module 2.4x over the maxed heat bar on its best skimmed descent.\",\n  \"checks\": [\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"one_band_all_pieces_inside\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"There is exactly one band, every catalog altitude falls inside it, and the params use only the sample keys bottom/middle/top.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"bands[] has one entry, 50,000-280,000 m. All 30 catalog altitudes lie inside: lowest torn_foil_blanket 52,500 m, highest armstrongs_module 277,000 m. Params use sample keys in three places - ablation.heat_index, ablation.cost_curve, ablation.optimal_skims - and each carries exactly {bottom, middle, top}; no fourth key and no band name appears anywhere in params.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_summary_agrees_with_catalog\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Each band_summary entry agrees with the debris array it summarises on piece_count, mean_mass_kg and fragile_fraction.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Thirds of the 230,000 m envelope split at 126,667 and 203,333 m. Bottom: 11 pieces, masses sum 218.0 kg, mean 19.818 -> reported 19.82; spawn weights sum 89, fragile weights 3+2+2+2=9, 9/89=0.10112 -> reported 0.1011. Middle: 10 pieces, masses sum 529.0, mean 52.90 -> reported 52.9; weights sum 69, fragile 3+4+3+2=12, 12/69=0.17391 -> reported 0.1739. Top: 9 pieces, masses sum 4,449.0, mean 494.33 -> reported 494.33; weights sum 40, fragile 3+2+3+2=10, 10/40=0.250 -> reported 0.25. Counts total 11+10+9=30, matching the array length. Midpoint altitudes 88,333 / 165,000 / 241,667 also reproduce.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"mean_mass_rises_with_altitude\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Mean piece mass rises third over third, so a better haul is a harder ride home.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"19.82 -> 52.90 -> 494.33 kg, strictly rising. Excluding armstrongs_module the top third still reads 849/8 = 106.1 kg, a 5.4x rise over the bottom third and a 2.0x rise over the middle, so the trend is not an artifact of the objective piece.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_share_rises_with_altitude\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile spawn share rises from roughly 1 in 10 at the bottom to roughly 1 in 4 at the top.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Spawn-weighted: 9/89 = 0.1011 (1 in 9.9), 12/69 = 0.1739 (1 in 5.8), 10/40 = 0.2500 (1 in 4). Both endpoints land on the target and the gradient is monotone.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"full_hold_doubles_mass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"A full hold roughly doubles the rocket's mass at starting storage size.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"cargo.base_slots 6 x measured kg_per_slot 17.36 = 104.1 kg against dry_mass_kg 105, giving (105+104.1)/105 = 1.992x. Well inside the 25% tolerance around 2.0. Measured cargo.full_hold_mass_ratio agrees at 1.99. Note this passes only because dry mass was set to meet it; see no_target_moved_to_pass.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_cost_curve_optimum_in_range\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Each cost_curve minimum equals the matching optimal_skims entry, and the curve is what the stated ablation model actually produces.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Model recomputed from params.ablation (base 8, growth 1.75, coeff 0.006, exp 1.8, skim_peak 34, mult [1,0.71,0.63,0.60]). Per-skim add = 0.006 x 34^1.8 = 3.426. Cycle tolls for k=0..3: 8, 22, 46.5, 89.375. Bottom (index 100): k=0 -> 8 + 0.006x100^1.8 (3981.1) = 8+23.89 = 31.89 vs 31.89; k=3 -> 89.375 + 3x3.426 + 0.006x60^1.8 (1587.4=9.52) = 109.18 vs 109.18. Top (index 158): k=0 -> 8 + 0.006x158^1.8 (9069=54.42) = 62.42 vs 62.42; k=1 -> 22 + 3.426 + 0.006x112.18^1.8 (4897=29.38) = 54.81 vs 54.80. Middle k=0 = 43.18 and k=1 = 44.42 also reproduce. Argmins: bottom 0, middle 0, top 1, matching optimal_skims exactly, and balance_notes states this same model. Caveat carried to the next check: substituting the flown multipliers moves the middle argmin from 0 (43.18) to 1 (36.24), so the curve is faithful to its coefficients but the coefficients are not the flown ones.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"heavy_descent_requires_multi_pass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"With entry pinned at the commit floor: the endgame haul cannot stay under the bar on one pass, can on two, and an empty ship and a full hold still can on one from anywhere in the band.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The floor is real: commit_floor_m 8,000 against atmosphere_top_m 43,000 constrains the entry by 35,000 m, so the check is live. Judged on committed_descents against the capacity ladder 222/224/226. Part 1 holds: module plunge_peak_heat 603.4 (bottom), 726.6 (middle), 909.9 (top), all far above 226. Part 2 fails: module best_skimmed_peak_heat 545.3 / 567 / 561, i.e. 2.4x the maxed 226, with survives=false and skim_saves_it=false in all four module rows - the finale is not flyable on two passes or on any pass count. Part 3 fails at base capacity: full-hold plunge from the top of the band peaks at 223.6 against heat_capacity 222 with survives=false and must_skim=true, so ordinary cargo is forced to aerobrake before the module is, which is the inversion the rule exists to prevent (it clears at tiers 1 and 2, 224/226, by 0.2 and 2.4 units). Empty ship plunges hold everywhere: 95.5 / 122 / 158.3. The rule is satisfiable by numbers - the flown window is capacity in (567, 603.4): above the hottest module skim (567, middle) and below the coolest module plunge (603.4, bottom), with full hold 223.6 and empty 158.3 comfortably inside - but that window is 6% wide and every tier of the ladder must fit in it.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Raise reentry.heat_capacity and the whole heat_shield tier ladder into the measured window (567, 603.4) so the module survives a skimmed descent at every tier while no tier reaches its 603.4 plunge.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_multiplier_matches_flight\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"The claimed skim_heat_multiplier matches the flown skim_heat_multiplier_measured at the top sample, entry by entry, within 0.10.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Claimed [1, 0.71, 0.63, 0.60] against flown top-sample [1, 0.382, 0.382, 0.382]. Deltas 0.000, 0.328, 0.248, 0.218 - three of four indices exceed the 0.10 tolerance, the worst by 3.3x. The flown array saturates at the first skim in every band (bottom [1, 0.716, 0.716, 0.716], middle [1, 0.519, 0.519, 0.519]), because at ballistic_coefficient staged 21.4 there is no orbit left to trim after one pass; the claimed array keeps falling through index 3, pricing a benefit that was measured not to exist. Every entry in cost_curve is computed off this array, so all twelve costs are wrong with it: at the middle sample the flown 0.519 drops the k=1 cost from 44.42 to 36.24 and moves that row's optimum from 0 to 1.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Adopt the flown values into ablation.skim_heat_multiplier - top [1, 0.382, 0.382, 0.382] - and recompute cost_curve and optimal_skims from them.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_pricing_and_thermal_fatigue\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"skim_heat_multiplier starts at 1.0, is non-increasing and flattens by its last two entries; cycle_toll_growth exceeds 1.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"skim_heat_multiplier [1, 0.71, 0.63, 0.60]: starts at 1.0, strictly non-increasing, and the step sizes decay 0.29 -> 0.08 -> 0.03, so the last two entries are 4.8% apart against a 29% first step - saturating in shape, if not as abruptly as the flight (which saturates entirely after index 1). cycle_toll_growth 1.75 > 1, so a k-skim descent costs 8 x (1.75^(k+1)-1)/0.75 - 8, 22, 46.5, 89.375 for k=0..3 - superlinear, and by 13 passes at the bottom the flown toll is 15,407% of a 100% plate. Feathering is bounded.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"parachute_speed_under_soft_threshold\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3\",\n      \"statement\": \"Full-hold parachute descent speed is under the soft-landing threshold but above about 3.5 m/s, so the Parachute upgrade is a real purchase.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Measured full-hold touchdown 4.40 m/s against landing.soft_landing_ms 5.0 - 12% of margin - and above the 3.5 floor. Recomputed: sqrt(2 x 209.1 x 9 / (1.5 x 1.8 x 72)) = sqrt(3763.8/194.4) = 4.40. The empty ship lands at 3.12 m/s, so the loaded-to-empty spread is 1.28 m/s and the canopy tiers have something to buy.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"parachute_speed_matches_flight\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3\",\n      \"statement\": \"The claimed descent speed is what the stated canopy actually flies, measured independently of the claim.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"parachute.independent = true, so the area was taken from params (72 m2 at Cd 1.8) and not solved backwards out of the claim. claimed_full_hold_ms 4.4 against measured_full_hold_ms 4.4, delta_ms 0.0, which is 0% of the claim against a 5% tolerance; terminal_velocity_ms 4.4 agrees and spread_across_bands_ms is 0. The number is a real agreement, not a tautology - but see no_target_moved_to_pass for where the 72 m2 came from.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"tow_fee_clamps_at_half\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"The tow fee is zero inside the free radius, linear beyond it, clamped at exactly 50% of haul value, and never negative.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"tow_fee = {free_radius_m 1200, max_fee_fraction 0.5, curve linear}. Fee is defined on max(0, d - 1200), so it is exactly 0 at and inside 1,200 m and cannot go negative for any d >= 0; max_fee_fraction is exactly 0.500, matching GDD 2.5's 50% clamp rather than approximating it; curve is 'linear' as specified, with no exponent or knee parameter present to bend it. On a 270-value floor haul the fee ceiling is 135, so a bad landing site is a bill and never a wiped run.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"lazy_run_breaks_even\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"Launch cost is at or below the value of three cheap pieces near the floor of the band.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"crumpled_panel_stack, small class, 56,000 m: multiplier 1 + (6,000/230,000) x 4.5 = 1.1174, value 40 x 1.1174 = 44.70. Three = 134.1 against economy.launch_cost 110, so the turnaround is 2.46 cheap pieces. Even the cheapest piece in the game, torn_foil_blanket at 52,500 m, prices at 40 x 1.0489 = 41.96 and three of those = 125.9 > 110. A full six-slot hold of floor scrap grosses roughly 270, clearing the 110 launch plus a 40% replate at 2/pct (80).\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"steady_tow_never_shears\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Under steady tow at the heaviest legal two-piece load, per-cable tension stays below magnet_hold_force_n with margin.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"eva.base_tether_slots 2; the two heaviest hand_tetherable pieces are impact_pitted_engine_bell 122 kg and cracked_command_module_hatch 112 kg (severed_boom_mast 165 kg is oversized and hand_tetherable false). a = 240 / (110 + 122 + 112) = 240/344 = 0.6977 m/s2. Worst single-cable tension = 122 x 0.6977 = 85.1 N against magnet_hold_force_n 900 - a margin of 10.6x. Single heaviest piece alone: a = 240/232 = 1.034, tension 126.2 N, still 7.1x under. Shear can only fire on latch or swing jerk, which is the rule.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"staging_is_one_way\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"No parameter anywhere reintroduces thrust after staging.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The only thrust-bearing params are flight.thrust_n 5200 and flight.rcs_thrust_n 260, both attached to the jettisoned stage's engine and the ship's RCS, plus eva.jetpack_thrust_n 240 which belongs to the astronaut and not the vehicle. There is no post-stage, reserve, restart or backup thrust key in flight, reentry, ablation or upgrades, and no upgrade entry targets an effect that would restore thrust to a staged ship - the twelve effects are fuel_capacity_kg, thrust_n, base_slots, heat_capacity, parachute_area_m2 and magnet_range_screens only.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_never_crushable_and_premium\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile pieces are never crushable at any compactor tier present, and the fragile premium makes one intact fragile piece worth several solid pieces of its class.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"cargo.compactor_tier = 1 is live. All twelve fragile entries carry uncrushable: true (torn_foil_blanket, cracked_solar_array, shredded_antenna_mesh, delaminated_radiator_fin, cracked_optics_barrel, shattered_star_tracker, cracked_ablative_nose_cone, split_avionics_rack, cracked_deep_space_dish, fused_ion_grid_assembly, shattered_cryo_dewar, cracked_command_module_hatch), plus armstrongs_module; every non-fragile entry reads uncrushable: false, and all three size classes carry fragile_uncrushable: true, so the exemption is machine-checkable per piece and per class. Premium: fragile_value_premium 3 x. shredded_antenna_mesh (medium, 93,000 m): 90 x 1.8413 x 3 = 497.1 against its nearest solid classmate cracked_battery_pallet (medium, 99,500 m): 90 x 1.9685 = 177.2 - a ratio of 2.81, so one intact fragile grab is worth close to three solid ones of the same class at the same altitude.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"oversized_rejects_hand_magnet\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Oversized junk rejects the hand magnet.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"size_classes.oversized.hand_tetherable = false, against true for small and medium. The three oversized entries - collapsed_dish_frame 48 kg at 86,500 m, buckled_pressure_bulkhead 96 kg at 160,000 m, severed_boom_mast 165 kg at 234,000 m - are one per third, and the first is met at 86,500 m inside the shipping slice, so the player learns the crane-cable limit early.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"twelve_upgrade_purchases\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"Twelve purchases: six parts, two tiers each, tier 2 dearer than tier 1, each tier improving the stat it names.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"12 entries, 6 parts x 2 tiers. Costs rise per part: fuel_tank 380->1450, thruster 420->1600, storage 520->1900, heat_shield 700->2400, parachute 600->2200, hand_magnet 300->1100. Stats improve monotonically from their base values: fuel_capacity_kg 70->88->105, thrust_n 5200->6200->7400, base_slots 6->8->10, heat_capacity 222->224->226, parachute_area_m2 72->320->1150, magnet_range_screens 0.125->0.19->0.28. No part is missing and no third tier appears.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"launch_survives_itself\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"The climb's peak heat stays under reentry.heat_capacity - the base ship survives its own launch.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"climb_peak_heat reads 0 for all three samples and climb_survivable is trivially true, but the reason is that the ship never gets fast enough to heat: all three ascents log reached=false, why='crashed on ascent', with arc apexes of 5,068 / 5,104 / 5,116 m and no crossing of the 583 m/s heating threshold. 0 < 222 is a vacuous pass over a vehicle that did not survive its launch, so the thermal claim is not confirmed and not refuted - it is unmeasured. Neither the GDD's 47.5 bar units nor the balancer's predicted ~23 exists in the data. The check cannot be honestly passed until an ascent completes.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Fix the ascent first - raise flight.fuel_capacity_kg and dry_mass_kg together so the climb completes - then re-read climb_peak_heat against reentry.heat_capacity.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"shipping_slice_reachable\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"The base ship reaches the band floor by arc or by orbit, and the bottom two thirds each carry at least one fragile piece and at least two size classes.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Neither route reaches. All three ascents report reached=false, arc_reached=false, orbit_reached=false, route=null, why='crashed on ascent'; arc apex peaks at 5,116 m against a band floor of 50,000 m - 10.2% of the altitude needed. arc_eva_window_s and arc_fuel_margin_pct are 0 in every case, against the GDD's 97 s window and 29.5% margin and the balancer's re-derived ~24%. The 2,196 m/s of ideal delta-v in balance_notes is not what the integrator flies: at 3.5 m2 frontal area, 1.5 kg/m3 sea-level density and 175 kg wet mass, drag ends the climb before gravity does. The catalog half of the check passes: the bottom third carries small, medium and oversized classes plus 4 fragile pieces, and the middle third carries small, medium and oversized plus 4 fragile pieces, so crushing and fragility are both met inside the slice.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Move flight.dry_mass_kg and flight.fuel_capacity_kg together toward the grid's reachable shape (the 435 fuel-margin-satisfying worlds cluster near dry 200 kg with 300-800 kg of tank and liftoff TWR 1.4-2.6, against the current 105/70 and TWR 3.3).\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"no_target_moved_to_pass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3\",\n      \"statement\": \"No rule passes only because a number the design document fixes in prose was moved to meet it.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"cargo.base_slots is correctly back at the GDD's 6 and eva.base_tether_slots at 2, so the previous slot-widening is fixed. But two other stated constants moved. (1) landing.parachute_area_m2 is 72 against GDD 2.3's explicit 'currently 630 m2 at Cd 1.8, predicting 4.4 m/s at base storage with a full hold' - the canopy is stated in the GDD precisely so the 4.4 m/s figure is a claim the flight can contradict. Shrinking it 8.75x preserves the 4.4 m/s output while the ship mass underneath it changed, so parachute_speed_matches_flight and parachute_speed_under_soft_threshold both pass against a target that was moved to keep them passing: at the GDD's 630 m2 the same 209.1 kg full hold would fly sqrt(3763.8/1701) = 1.49 m/s, far under the 3.5 floor. (2) flight.dry_mass_kg 105 was chosen to make full_hold_doubles_mass read 1.99x, and the balancer says so in its own notes; the consequence is that GDD 2.6's 'roughly doubling the ship's mass' for the 3,600 kg module reads 34x instead of 2x, and the staged ballistic coefficient falls to 105/(1.4 x 3.5) = 21.4 against the Researcher's floor of 100 and the flight's own aerobraking floor of ~50 - which is what removed the skim mechanic and crashed the ascent. Two GDD-stated quantities cannot both be true of this vehicle, and the resolution taken was to move them.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Restore landing.parachute_area_m2 toward the GDD's stated 630 m2 and let the descent speed be measured rather than pinned, and raise flight.dry_mass_kg toward a ballistic coefficient above 100 - then report the full-hold-doubling rule as a catalog conflict rather than satisfying it from dry mass.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"observations\": [\n    \"The heat-shield ladder is 222 / 224 / 226 - a 1.8% span across the whole track. A player buying the tier-2 composite for 2,400 gains 4 bar units, which no cockpit readout will make legible; the shield's felt progression is entirely its mass and plate, not the number the GDD asked it to sell.\",\n    \"At heat_capacity 222 the top-of-band full hold already cannot plunge (223.6, survives=false, must_skim=true) while the module cannot come home by any route. Mandatory aerobraking currently sits on ordinary cargo instead of on the finale - the inverse of what GDD 2.2 wanted, where 'an empty ship and a full hold still plunge freely'.\",\n    \"The module lands at 18.58 m/s in all twelve descent cells against soft_landing_ms 5. The 1,150 m2 tier-2 canopy is priced against a predicted 4.63 m/s the simulator does not reproduce, so even if the heat problem were fixed the pristine delivery would be unreachable on touchdown speed. Worth deciding whether the module's own degradation threshold, not the ship's damage line, is what should carry it.\",\n    \"The module's cheapest descent burns 334.8-383.4% of a 100% plate at every band position. The finale is out of reach on plate as well as on capacity, and those are separate levers - fixing heat_capacity alone will not fix it.\",\n    \"Unstaged braking peaks at 417 (bottom) and 401 (middle) against any tier of the ladder, so GDD 2.2's 'real choice' between staged and unstaged passes is always death on the unstaged branch. The 3x multiplier is a mechanic the ship cannot pay for at this capacity.\",\n    \"Feathering is bounded economically but not physically: pass_counts_reachable goes to 25 at top-of-band full hold, and peak heat is nearly flat across them (94 at one pass, 108 at twenty-five). The player gets no thermal signal that the twenty-fifth pass differs from the second - only an ablation bill they cannot see until the plate is gone.\",\n    \"economy.size_class_base_value.oversized = 320 prices a class that no hold can ever contain, since oversized is hand_tetherable false and the crane magnet is cut. Harmless, but it is a live number in the value table that nothing in the shipping slice can read.\",\n    \"The measured skim study saturates after the first skim in every band, which means skims 2 and 3 are not merely mispriced but unpriceable at this vehicle's ballistic coefficient. Any future revision of skim_heat_multiplier should expect a two-entry array in practice, however many entries the schema wants.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":138043,"ttft_stream_ms":2019,"time_to_request_ms":384,"type":"result","duration_ms":250042,"uuid":"5800ad84-c07b-4d8d-ba0e-72bdadb5cf13"}
